userStories.description:
**Goal** — Add a nullable `brief` field to Project, editable through the existing `PATCH /api/projects/:slug` and returned on project responses.

**Motivation** — Nothing in koda holds a short project description an admin curates (`Project.description` is a one-line label and is empty on koda-wk); the always-loaded brief is the cheapest, highest-value grounding for a brainstorm agent.

**Approach** — 1c-D10 (who edits the brief): the brief rides the existing `PATCH /api/projects/:slug`, which requires `@RequiredPermission('ADMIN')` (`projects.controller.ts:100`). No new route. `ProjectsService.update` passes `brief` into `updateBySlug` as it passes `description` (`projects.service.ts:156`); an empty string is stored as given and renders as `(none)`. 1c-D6 (part): `Project.brief` is at most 3000 characters (validated on update). Add migration `apps/api/prisma/migrations/20261019090000_project_brief/migration.sql`, the DTO and domain fields and the e2e coverage; regenerate `openapi.json` with `bun run api:export-spec` and the generated clients with `bun run generate` (never hand-edit generated files).

**Scope** — In: Prisma schema + migration, ProjectDomain, toDomain, UpdateProjectDto, ProjectResponseDto, ProjectsService.update, projects.controller.spec.ts literal (gains `brief: null`), e2e PATCH cases (200 echoed, 3001-char 400, non-ADMIN member 403), openapi.json + client regeneration. Out: rendering the brief into instructions (US-003); the web editor (US-006); any new route or permission (editing stays on `PATCH /api/projects/:slug`, global ADMIN).

**Interface**
```
// schema.prisma  model Project:   brief String? @db.Text
// ProjectDomain:                  brief?: string | null   (optional so existing test literals stay valid)
// UpdateProjectDto.brief:
//   @IsOptional() @IsString({ message: '$t(common.validation.isString)' }) @MaxLength(3000, { message: '$t(common.validation.maxLength)' })
//   @ApiPropertyOptional({ nullable: true })
// ProjectResponseDto.from(...) maps brief (string | null)
```

**Goal** — Add the `KnowledgeTools` group with the `koda_memory_search` tool, backed by a new case-insensitive `text` filter on project memory.

**Motivation** — The memory koda already records is reachable only from the REST API and the CLI, not from a thread.

**Approach** — 1c-R1: `koda_memory_search` filters memory items by a case-insensitive text match over `subject`, `predicate` and `object`. No embeddings. Umbrella rulings: tools run on the API as the thread's creator; the executor owns the project scope; a foreign id answers `not_found`.

1c-D7 (tool group): both knowledge tools live in a new injectable `KnowledgeTools` (`apps/api/src/fleet/assistant/tools/knowledge-tools.ts`) that registers them in `onModuleInit`, as `TicketTools` does. Their descriptors are `KNOWLEDGE_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS`, appended to `KODA_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS`. `AssistantModule` imports `MemoryModule` (no cycle: no module reachable from `MemoryModule` imports a fleet module). This story creates the file with `koda_memory_search`; US-005 adds `koda_timeline`.

1c-D8 (memory through the service): `koda_memory_search` calls `MemoryGovernanceService.getProjectMemory`, which passes a new `text` field of `ProjectMemoryQuery` to `PrismaMemoryItemRepository.findByProjectMemory`. The repository adds the text match as its own `AND` term (an `OR` over `subject`, `predicate`, `object`, each `contains` with `mode: 'insensitive'`), so it never replaces the existing TTL `OR` (`prisma-memory-item.repository.ts:260`). The `AND` term is added only when `text` is set, so the `where` objects asserted by `memory-item-repository.spec.ts` for queries without `text` are unchanged. A `text` that is only whitespace is rejected by the tool's parser (`text`).

Tool contract: `AssistantTool<I>` `{ descriptor, permission, parse(input): I, execute(ctx, input): Promise<string> }`, `ToolContext` `{ projectId, projectSlug, actor, threadId, jobId, now, data(text) }`, `ToolInputError(reason)` (`assistant-tool.types.ts`). `TicketTools` (`tools/ticket-tools.ts`) is the pattern, including `PAGE_SIZE = 20` and the `more:` line.

Tool: `koda_memory_search` — Input `text?` (1-100 chars), `kind?` (`FACT`, `DECISION`, `PREFERENCE`, `CONSTRAINT`, `INCIDENT_PATTERN`), `status?` (`active`, `superseded`, `rejected`), `page?` (integer at least 1); permission member; service `MemoryGovernanceService.getProjectMemory` (order `updatedAt`, pages of 20); fenced fields subject, predicate, object. It emits per item a line `memory:<id> <kind> <status> confidence <confidence with 2 decimals> updated <ISO time>` followed by the fenced text `<subject> <predicate> <object>` (object omitted when null). With `status` absent the query passes no status, so the service applies its default (active and not expired by `ttlAt`). No match, including a page past the last, gives the single line `no memory items`. A next page gives `more: call koda_memory_search with page <n+1>`. Invalid input -> `invalid_input: <field>`.

**Scope** — In: knowledge-tools.ts (+spec), KNOWLEDGE_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS in koda-tool-descriptors.ts appended to KODA_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS, assistant.module.ts (import MemoryModule, provide KnowledgeTools), assistant.module.spec.ts (may add stubs to global-stubs.module.ts for MemoryModule's graph, keeping its one assertion that it resolves AssistantToolExecutor), memory-item-repository.ts, prisma-memory-item.repository.ts, MemoryGovernanceService passthrough. Out: koda_timeline (US-005); KB search; embeddings.

**Interface**
```typescript
// memory-item-repository.ts
interface ProjectMemoryQuery { /* existing */ text?: string }
// knowledge-tools.ts
@Injectable() export class KnowledgeTools implements OnModuleInit { onModuleInit(): void /* registers koda_memory_search */ }
// koda-tool-descriptors.ts
export const KNOWLEDGE_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS: ToolDescriptor[]  // appended to KODA_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS
```
